export anchor PluginCapabilities:
    description:
        A plugin is code from somewhere else running on the user's machine, so
        it may do only what its declaration lists. Reading the compiled
        project is always allowed, since it changes nothing. Everything else
        is a capability, and anything not listed is refused.

        ```piton
        \\\
        capabilities:
            process: [claude, codex]
            files: read
            temporaryFiles: true
        \\\
        ```

    process:
        The programs the plugin may run, by name, looked up on PATH. Only
        these can be started, and only these can be looked for on PATH. A
        program is named as it is run, so `claude` allows `claude` and
        nothing else.

    files:
        read lets the plugin read files inside the project root; none, the
        default, doesn't. A path that leads outside the project is refused
        either way. A plugin never writes files in the project: what it
        produces goes to stdout and stderr.

    temporaryFiles:
        true lets the plugin write temporary files, outside the project, each
        removed when its command ends, like the file OpenCode reads its
        instructions from.

    refusal:
        A refused operation throws in the plugin, saying what it tried and
        which capability would allow it, like "plugin `hello` may not run
        `ls`: its declaration does not list it under capabilities.process".
        The plugin can catch it; uncaught, the command fails with it.

    unknown:
        A capability that isn't one of these makes the declaration invalid,
        so a plugin never runs believing it has something it doesn't.

    visibility:
        `piton plugins` shows what each plugin may do, so what a project's
        plugins can reach is one command away.

    whatItIsNot:
        Capabilities limit what the plugin's own code does. A program it is
        allowed to run is not sandboxed by them: an agentic tool started by
        the test plugin can do what that tool allows, which is why the test
        plugin starts each tool in its own read-only mode where it has one.
